From c94831b592b82ef3934d7b22d91488970008cb34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 06:12:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 05/24] Read the IOSurface mapper registers without the device
 lock

A vCPU reading a mapper register took the device lock, and so waited out
whatever the drain worker was doing under it: a whole tranche, 8.2 s
when that tranche compiled a pipeline, with QEMU's BQL held throughout.
All five reads, ring base, capacity, descriptor table, producer and
consumer, are plain state with no side effect.

Serve them from IosfcLive, a set of atomics shared with the registry
slot as the GFX consumer counter already is. Every writer of the
registers publishes them under the device lock: the register write
itself before it returns, drain_iosfc once it has advanced the consumer
and before it queues the mapper interrupt, so the guest's handler finds
the consumer caught up, and a device reset with the values it cleared.

The writes are unchanged and stay synchronous on the vCPU that makes
them. A producer write reads that vCPU's registers and walks guest
kernel memory through its MMU, and the guest finds the request done
when the write returns; the configuration and consumer writes are rare
and stay ordered before it. With the QEMU side dispatching the window
without the BQL, a writer that waits for the device lock no longer
holds the BQL while it does.

Count each vCPU's mapper register accesses, entry to return, split into
reads and writes (vcpuN_iosfc_read_call, vcpuN_iosfc_write_call on the
device_lock_hold lines). While QEMU dispatched the window under the
BQL, that was how long the vCPU held it. The read holder leaves the
census, since reads no longer take the lock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 96177a523795bef122c4257f6d51156ed3f4efdb)
